**Definition 4.1 (the entire live algebra).** Let

$$
\mathcal B=\bigoplus_{\alpha=1}^N M_{n_\alpha},\qquad
R=\dim_{\mathbb R}\mathcal B_{\rm sa}=\sum_\alpha n_\alpha^2,
\qquad D=\sum_\alpha n_\alpha<\infty.
\tag{FH.10}
$$

The usual sum of matrix traces is used. All jointly accessible
history-bearing inputs to a future live operation factor through this
one fixed algebra: specimen, archives, references and receiver shares,
source/epoch and programme fields, clock latches, calibration, permissions,
controller, selectors and every input used for updates. An installed
source-dependent configuration, shared seed with source-bearing joint
correlations, readable environment or continuously valued source-sensitive
setting is an input too. A source-independent marginal does not exempt
its joint correlations. A genuinely inaccessible spectator may stay
outside $\mathcal B$ only if it never assists later selection or processing.

For each actual original history $h=(x,\beta,m)$, acquisition produces
an arbitrary positive live block $L_h\in\mathcal B$ with
$\operatorname{tr}L_h\leq1$. Every acquisition outcome with a live
successor, including a refusal that leaves the specimen live and may
later be reconsidered, is included jointly in $L_h$. No product form,
phase/winding independence, purity, known ensemble, readable height label,
continuity of $h\mapsto L_h$ or common encoder is assumed. This grants
even hypothetical arbitrary encodings; it is an impossibility relaxation,
not a construction of such acquisition.

The remaining acquisition mass is permanently stopped or aborted and
cannot assist a live branch. Its exact actual archive remains in the full
output, possibly outside a finite algebra. For necessary lower tests only,
forget that archive and map these sectors to one absorbing symbol $\bot$:

$$
\sigma_h=L_h\oplus(1-\operatorname{tr}L_h)|\bot\rangle\langle\bot|
\quad\text{in}\quad\widehat{\mathcal B}=\mathcal B\oplus\mathbb C.
\tag{FH.11}
$$

This is normalized. Forgetting stopped archives contracts the comparison
distance; it neither erases them from the actual contract nor resamples
their events. If a supposedly stopped record can control a surviving
specimen on the same branch, it belongs in $L_h$ instead.

